  "summary": "The dollar is breaking higher against the euro, sterling, the Australian dollar and the kiwi, while U.S. yields and the secured-funding strip are higher. The S&P 500 is 7323, gold is 3742, and crude is elevated but well off the prior spike, with WTI at 83.26 and Brent at 91.95. Fed minutes showed officials still open to more hikes and worried that AI could add to inflation, and Governor Waller talked about a possible half-point move. Treasury yields were inching higher into the morning after that session. The Bank of Japan governor remained open to tightening despite weak consumption. New Zealand's governor cited above-target inflation and an uneven recovery; sovereign yields there are unavailable, while kiwi spot is still a live price. Canada retail trade and Japan CPI are next, then Australian prices and U.S. growth data, ahead of the December central-bank decisions.",
